WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.625 --> 00:00:02.168
For millennia, humans
have turned to plants

00:00:02.252 --> 00:00:05.505
to heal the sick and wounded,
to ward against evil

00:00:05.588 --> 00:00:07.424
and grant magical powers.

00:00:07.507 --> 00:00:10.468
But what happens when plants
themselves become conscious

00:00:10.552 --> 00:00:12.011
and turn killers?

00:00:12.095 --> 00:00:14.806
More monstrous associations
link vegetation

00:00:14.889 --> 00:00:17.267
to malevolent sorcery,
demonic influence,

00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:19.686
or evil and, typically,
human-like qualities.

00:00:19.769 --> 00:00:22.397
Characteristics like
extremely large size,

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:24.983
enhanced mobility,
including sentient movement,

00:00:25.066 --> 00:00:27.110
an insatiable carnivorous diet,

00:00:27.193 --> 00:00:30.697
and poisonous excretions
can all make a harmless plant

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:32.574
into a terrifying monster.

00:00:32.657 --> 00:00:34.367
The most common unifying element

00:00:34.451 --> 00:00:37.078
of terror among these
so-called monstrous plants?

00:00:37.162 --> 00:00:40.582
Their ability and
desire to kill humans.

00:00:40.665 --> 00:00:41.666
[energetic music]

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:45.587
I'm Dr. Emily Zarka
and this is Monstrum.

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:48.631
Poisonous plants like hemlock
and belladonna are lethal

00:00:48.715 --> 00:00:50.592
to humans and have
been employed as such.

00:00:50.675 --> 00:00:53.553
Given these potentially
devastating consequences,

00:00:53.636 --> 00:00:55.597
some of this vegetation
was associated

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:56.973
with mystical properties,

00:00:57.056 --> 00:01:00.143
especially if they also
possessed an unusual appearance.

00:01:00.226 --> 00:01:02.854
Orchids were associated with
witchcraft, deadly nightshade

00:01:02.937 --> 00:01:04.481
and mandrakes with Satan.

00:01:04.564 --> 00:01:07.609
These real-world deadly plants
helped inspire the folklore

00:01:07.692 --> 00:01:09.235
around their monstrous cousins,

00:01:09.319 --> 00:01:11.571
the ones that kill
consciously on their own.

00:01:11.654 --> 00:01:14.032
One early example appears
in the medieval period

00:01:14.115 --> 00:01:16.367
in a natural history
encyclopedia.

00:01:16.451 --> 00:01:17.786
Published in 1491,

00:01:17.869 --> 00:01:21.372
Meydenbach's Ortus Sanitatis
includes a description

00:01:21.456 --> 00:01:23.958
and illustration
of the bausor tree.

00:01:24.042 --> 00:01:26.920
It was said to exude toxic
fumes that caused death

00:01:27.003 --> 00:01:30.715
for miles around for any
living thing, even humans.

00:01:30.799 --> 00:01:34.886
Some 250 years later in
1783, an article appeared

00:01:34.969 --> 00:01:38.723
in "The London Magazine" by
Dutch surgeon, N.P. Foersch,

00:01:38.807 --> 00:01:41.559
who told of the existence
of a strange tree,

00:01:41.643 --> 00:01:43.394
the Javanese Upas.

00:01:43.478 --> 00:01:45.438
He alleged that on
the island of Java,

00:01:45.522 --> 00:01:47.524
there existed a tree
whose biology was

00:01:47.607 --> 00:01:50.693
so sensational, it had
previously been dismissed

00:01:50.777 --> 00:01:53.071
as myth by European naturalists.

00:01:53.154 --> 00:01:54.572
According to Foersch,

00:01:54.656 --> 00:01:57.867
indigenous peoples called
this plant the Bohun Upas.

00:01:57.951 --> 00:01:59.577
With the permission of the
government and the help

00:01:59.661 --> 00:02:02.664
of two Malayan priests,
Foersch claims that he was able

00:02:02.747 --> 00:02:06.334
to observe the tree himself,
from a distance, of course,

00:02:06.417 --> 00:02:09.629
as not only the bark of the tree
but the fumes emanating

00:02:09.712 --> 00:02:12.757
from its resin are
lethal to all life.

00:02:12.841 --> 00:02:14.592
The only humans
who approach the tree

00:02:14.676 --> 00:02:18.346
are condemned criminals
given a chance at clemency

00:02:18.429 --> 00:02:21.724
if they can successfully harvest
the poisonous secretions.

00:02:21.808 --> 00:02:23.476
The existence of Foersch
and his record

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:25.770
of the tree are still contested.

00:02:25.854 --> 00:02:27.438
Historical documents show

00:02:27.522 --> 00:02:29.274
that a surgeon named
John Nichols Foersch

00:02:29.357 --> 00:02:31.067
did indeed exist,

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:34.028
and that the man lived
in the East Indies.

00:02:34.112 --> 00:02:35.572
This surgeon expressed interest

00:02:35.655 --> 00:02:37.532
in writing a book
about his travels.

00:02:37.615 --> 00:02:41.161
So it is possible he
concocted the Bohun Upas story

00:02:41.244 --> 00:02:42.537
as a publicity stunt.

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:44.581
However,
other historians attribute

00:02:44.664 --> 00:02:46.249
"The London Magazine" article

00:02:46.332 --> 00:02:49.586
to an anonymous source who
spun the tale from scratch.

00:02:49.669 --> 00:02:51.671
Either way, there is a real tree

00:02:51.754 --> 00:02:56.050
with poisonous sap we now
call the Antiaris toxicaria,

00:02:56.134 --> 00:02:58.511
that was undoubtedly
well-known among inhabitants

00:02:58.595 --> 00:03:00.513
of its natural habitats.

00:03:00.597 --> 00:03:02.974
But it wasn't until
sensationalized accounts

00:03:03.057 --> 00:03:04.976
like this one
in the 18th century

00:03:05.059 --> 00:03:06.978
that this deadly plant
was turned

00:03:07.061 --> 00:03:09.063
into a plant that killed.

00:03:09.147 --> 00:03:11.983
Many such stories weren't
just tabloid fodder,

00:03:12.066 --> 00:03:14.527
but were nonetheless
just as sensational.

00:03:14.611 --> 00:03:18.573
Naturalist Erasmus Darwin
wrote of the Javanese Upas tree

00:03:18.656 --> 00:03:21.910
and its poisonous vapor
in his 1789 text,

00:03:21.993 --> 00:03:23.161
The Botanic Garden,

00:03:23.244 --> 00:03:24.370
offering the compelling image

00:03:24.454 --> 00:03:27.332
of a barren land
surrounding the plant,

00:03:27.415 --> 00:03:31.085
punctuated only by the
skeletons of men and animals.

00:03:31.169 --> 00:03:33.880
While not the man-eating plant
trope we've come to recognize

00:03:33.963 --> 00:03:36.341
as characteristic
of killer flora,

00:03:36.424 --> 00:03:40.094
these early literary appearances
planted imaginative seeds

00:03:40.178 --> 00:03:42.347
that would flourish
in the 19th century.

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:45.934
As the steam engine advanced
and expediated trade routes,

00:03:46.017 --> 00:03:48.102
transport of plants from
one side of the globe

00:03:48.186 --> 00:03:51.522
to the other became more
successful in the 19th century.

00:03:51.606 --> 00:03:53.733
Improvements in glass
and steel production used

00:03:53.816 --> 00:03:55.693
to build greenhouses
and the invention

00:03:55.777 --> 00:03:57.570
of the hothouse made cultivation

00:03:57.654 --> 00:03:59.572
of foreign
plant species possible

00:03:59.656 --> 00:04:01.783
in previously unfit climates.

00:04:01.866 --> 00:04:04.118
Alongside this
perennial privilege,

00:04:04.202 --> 00:04:07.580
plants as intruders was an
increasingly dominant narrative

00:04:07.664 --> 00:04:08.790
in the 1800s.

00:04:08.873 --> 00:04:12.293
Wild and unkempt at best
or sentient and mobile

00:04:12.377 --> 00:04:14.712
in the most
horrifying variations,

00:04:14.796 --> 00:04:17.507
the idea of encroaching
vegetation appeared

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:19.425
across literary genres.

00:04:19.509 --> 00:04:20.927
Fictional travel accounts

00:04:21.010 --> 00:04:23.972
increasingly featured exotic
killer plants; Gothic tales

00:04:24.055 --> 00:04:27.600
and short stories of weird
vegetation became popular,

00:04:27.684 --> 00:04:29.352
meaning an influx
in the perception

00:04:29.435 --> 00:04:31.604
that flora could
consume animal flesh,

00:04:31.688 --> 00:04:33.731
including the occasional human.

00:04:33.815 --> 00:04:36.067
This is in part due
to a scientific text

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:37.610
and another Darwin.

00:04:37.694 --> 00:04:41.489
Charles Darwin's 1875 book,
Insectivorous Plants,

00:04:41.572 --> 00:04:44.367
popularized the
flesh-eating plant.

00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:46.286
The vibrantly illustrated text

00:04:46.369 --> 00:04:48.538
details the
naturalist's experiments

00:04:48.621 --> 00:04:50.748
with carnivorous plants,
their movements,

00:04:50.832 --> 00:04:54.335
digestive processes,
and favorite food sources.

00:04:54.419 --> 00:04:57.880
Nature, it became apparent,
supported natural monsters,

00:04:57.964 --> 00:05:00.550
and inevitably,
fiction writers would expand

00:05:00.633 --> 00:05:03.469
upon their existence
in dramatic ways.

00:05:03.553 --> 00:05:05.555
Fake accounts of
man-eating plants emerged

00:05:05.638 --> 00:05:08.224
in publications around
that same decade.

00:05:08.307 --> 00:05:10.018
Perhaps the most
widely popularized

00:05:10.101 --> 00:05:13.730
of these tales was "The
Man-Eating Tree of Madagascar."

00:05:13.813 --> 00:05:15.815
On April 28th, 1874,

00:05:15.898 --> 00:05:18.026
The New York World published
an incredible story

00:05:18.109 --> 00:05:21.529
detailing the account of
Karl Leche's discovery

00:05:21.612 --> 00:05:24.699
of a tentacled carnivorous
man-eating pitcher plant

00:05:24.782 --> 00:05:26.451
in Madagascar.

00:05:26.534 --> 00:05:28.911
He claimed the tree was
worshiped by locals,

00:05:28.995 --> 00:05:31.414
secreted a hallucinogenic sap,

00:05:31.497 --> 00:05:34.792
and used vine-like
tentacles to trap its victim

00:05:34.876 --> 00:05:37.754
while pointed leaves
squashed them to death.

00:05:37.837 --> 00:05:39.881
More than ten years later,
in 1888,

00:05:39.964 --> 00:05:42.467
the story was
revealed as a hoax.

00:05:42.550 --> 00:05:44.844
But by that time,
fiction writers were filling

00:05:44.927 --> 00:05:48.181
in the gaps of imaginations,
turning newspaper stories

00:05:48.264 --> 00:05:50.475
into sensational
horror narratives.

00:05:50.558 --> 00:05:53.019
Take Arthur Conan Doyle's
The Americans' Tale,

00:05:53.102 --> 00:05:56.147
a short story about a giant
carnivorous Venus flytrap

00:05:56.230 --> 00:05:57.398
in Montana;

00:05:57.482 --> 00:05:59.776
or Phil Robinson's
The Man-Eating Tree,

00:05:59.859 --> 00:06:01.903
where an African
man-sucking tree

00:06:01.986 --> 00:06:04.947
with beautiful golden fruit
serves as a lure

00:06:05.031 --> 00:06:06.574
to grab nearby prey

00:06:06.657 --> 00:06:10.203
with its boughs before using
large vampire-like leaves

00:06:10.286 --> 00:06:12.455
to suck the blood
of its victims.

00:06:12.538 --> 00:06:15.500
In Lucy H. Hooper's
1889 Carnivorine,

00:06:15.583 --> 00:06:18.377
the mad scientist trope
enters into the genre

00:06:18.461 --> 00:06:19.712
with a botanist who attempts

00:06:19.796 --> 00:06:21.964
to prove an
evolutionary connection

00:06:22.048 --> 00:06:24.050
between plant and animal.

00:06:24.133 --> 00:06:25.968
As a result of his experiments,

00:06:26.052 --> 00:06:28.137
the plant develops
paddle-like feet,

00:06:28.221 --> 00:06:31.516
making it mobile, and a taste
for human flesh,

00:06:31.599 --> 00:06:34.727
resulting in an
uncontrollable killer monster.

00:06:34.811 --> 00:06:35.978
And in 1891,

00:06:36.062 --> 00:06:39.690
the horrible plant called the
Devil's Snare was introduced

00:06:39.774 --> 00:06:42.777
by an American naturalist
living in Nicaragua.

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:45.404
Like a weeping willow
without foliage,

00:06:45.488 --> 00:06:48.533
the thick dark black vines
appear like a mass

00:06:48.616 --> 00:06:51.869
of interlacing
thick muscular fibers.

00:06:51.953 --> 00:06:53.871
Vines that suck blood,
of course.

00:06:53.955 --> 00:06:56.666
In 1894, H.G. Wells'
The Flowering

00:06:56.749 --> 00:06:58.751
of the Strange Orchid
tells the tale

00:06:58.835 --> 00:07:01.712
of a British orchid collector
whose newest procurement

00:07:01.796 --> 00:07:05.341
from the Indies, whose buds
resemble little white fingers,

00:07:05.424 --> 00:07:09.262
ends up nearly strangling him
using sucker-adorned roots

00:07:09.345 --> 00:07:10.805
to drink his blood.

00:07:10.888 --> 00:07:12.473
Noticing a trend here?

00:07:12.557 --> 00:07:16.102
The plants in these narratives
come from outside of Europe.

00:07:16.185 --> 00:07:18.563
Many killer plant stories
from the 19th century

00:07:18.646 --> 00:07:21.107
and beyond represent
the unconquered

00:07:21.190 --> 00:07:23.317
and uncontrollable
natural world.

00:07:23.401 --> 00:07:25.153
Colonialism plays a role here

00:07:25.236 --> 00:07:27.864
as writers turned
passively poisonous flora

00:07:27.947 --> 00:07:30.449
into predatory
man-eating beasts,

00:07:30.533 --> 00:07:33.536
painting the foreign as
dangerous and terrifying

00:07:33.619 --> 00:07:36.330
as a means to affirm
Western superiority.

00:07:36.414 --> 00:07:37.790
At the turn of the 20th century,

00:07:37.874 --> 00:07:39.750
plants were
increasingly considered

00:07:39.834 --> 00:07:42.044
with curiosity,
suspicion, and fear

00:07:42.128 --> 00:07:44.755
in both scientific texts
and horror fiction.

00:07:44.839 --> 00:07:49.177
The 1920s and '30s saw a swell
in monstrous plant narratives.

00:07:49.260 --> 00:07:51.512
Pulp science fiction was
thriving at the time,

00:07:51.596 --> 00:07:54.182
but so were debates about
teaching evolutionary theory

00:07:54.265 --> 00:07:56.893
in schools, ones that
made national headlines

00:07:56.976 --> 00:07:58.311
with The Scopes Trial.

00:07:58.394 --> 00:08:00.271
Fostered by the
uncertainty of the Cold War

00:08:00.354 --> 00:08:02.899
and the rapidly evolving
scientific discoveries

00:08:02.982 --> 00:08:04.609
of the '50s and '60s,

00:08:04.692 --> 00:08:06.569
monster plants
popped up regularly

00:08:06.652 --> 00:08:08.571
on screen and in books.

00:08:08.654 --> 00:08:11.324
Speculation surrounding
the potential impacts

00:08:11.407 --> 00:08:14.410
of nuclear mutation,
extraterrestrial life,

00:08:14.493 --> 00:08:17.830
bio-weapons, and chemical
herbicides present themselves

00:08:17.914 --> 00:08:19.624
in monstrous plant narratives.

00:08:19.707 --> 00:08:21.500
In the famous
The Day of the Triffids,

00:08:21.584 --> 00:08:25.296
a postapocalyptic 1951 novel
by John Wyndham,

00:08:25.379 --> 00:08:29.217
mysterious plants are a metaphor
for fears of the Cold War.

00:08:29.300 --> 00:08:31.844
When a comet blinds most
of the world's population,

00:08:31.928 --> 00:08:33.846
humankind becomes vulnerable

00:08:33.930 --> 00:08:36.224
to walking stinging plants
first bred

00:08:36.307 --> 00:08:39.685
by the Soviet Union during
genetic experimentation

00:08:39.769 --> 00:08:42.480
and accidentally
released into the world.

00:08:42.563 --> 00:08:45.274
The triffid plants
evolve to prey on humans,

00:08:45.358 --> 00:08:46.943
using the global catastrophe

00:08:47.026 --> 00:08:48.569
to dominate the planet.

00:08:48.653 --> 00:08:51.739
That same year, in the film
The Thing from Another World,

00:08:51.822 --> 00:08:55.493
killer extraterrestrial plants
wreak havoc on Earth.

00:08:55.576 --> 00:08:59.080
Inspired in part by growing
reports of UFOs and aliens,

00:08:59.163 --> 00:09:02.541
as well as rumored accounts of
Soviet forces in the Arctic,

00:09:02.625 --> 00:09:05.920
the film's plant-based
alien life form is defrosted

00:09:06.003 --> 00:09:09.382
by military personnel and
scientists in the North Pole.

00:09:09.465 --> 00:09:12.218
If this plot sounds familiar,
that's because it is.

00:09:12.301 --> 00:09:15.972
Both this movie and
1982's The Thing were based

00:09:16.055 --> 00:09:19.976
on a 1938 novel
by John W. Campbell, Jr.

00:09:20.059 --> 00:09:23.062
In 1956, Invasion of
the Body Snatchers

00:09:23.145 --> 00:09:26.023
would make monstrous
plants more terrifying

00:09:26.107 --> 00:09:28.818
by giving them the
ability to clone humans.

00:09:28.901 --> 00:09:30.945
Then The Woman Eater paid homage

00:09:31.028 --> 00:09:34.991
to the 19th century sensational
man-killer plant narratives

00:09:35.074 --> 00:09:37.660
with a carnivorous tree
worshiped by locals.

00:09:37.743 --> 00:09:42.039
And 1960 brought us the hapless
Skid Row florist Seymour,

00:09:42.123 --> 00:09:44.959
who cultivates a blood-thirsty
potted plant,

00:09:45.042 --> 00:09:47.670
hoping to use the novelty to
advance his own standing

00:09:47.753 --> 00:09:50.256
with his boss and propagate
his own love life.

00:09:50.339 --> 00:09:52.717
In the original 1960s
version of the film,

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:55.553
Audrey, Jr, defeats
and eats Seymour.

00:09:55.636 --> 00:09:57.471
Audiences of the 1986 version

00:09:57.555 --> 00:09:59.307
of Little Shop watch
a more pleasant

00:09:59.390 --> 00:10:02.310
and reassuring ending,
when it is instead Seymour

00:10:02.393 --> 00:10:04.020
who defeats Audrey, Jr.

00:10:04.103 --> 00:10:06.605
Audrey, Jr. immortalizes
its victim's faces

00:10:06.689 --> 00:10:09.817
in its blooming flowers,
which the historian in me

00:10:09.900 --> 00:10:11.610
can't help but interpret
as a callback

00:10:11.694 --> 00:10:12.987
to the Wak Wak tree.

00:10:13.070 --> 00:10:16.532
First recorded in the late
14th century Book of Surprises,

00:10:16.615 --> 00:10:20.494
the bizarre tree appears in
multiple Indo Arabian legends,

00:10:20.578 --> 00:10:23.039
where it is said to
grow either human heads

00:10:23.122 --> 00:10:26.083
or full-bodied humans
amongst its branches.

00:10:26.167 --> 00:10:29.253
In the 1970s and '80s,
advances in bioengineering led

00:10:29.337 --> 00:10:31.881
to a flurry of dangerous
flora narratives

00:10:31.964 --> 00:10:34.300
in response to anxieties
about the consequences

00:10:34.383 --> 00:10:35.634
of bioengineering.

00:10:35.718 --> 00:10:37.762
Venus Flytrap returns
to the premise

00:10:37.845 --> 00:10:40.556
Hooper first introduced
in the 19th century,

00:10:40.639 --> 00:10:43.225
when a botanist looking to
prove human life evolved

00:10:43.309 --> 00:10:46.062
from plants accidentally
creates a monster.

00:10:46.145 --> 00:10:49.774
In 1985, "Super Mario"'s
piranha plants, inspired in part

00:10:49.857 --> 00:10:53.694
by the real-life invasive plant
species Hydnora africana,

00:10:53.778 --> 00:10:57.073
immortalized killer plants
in video games.

00:10:57.156 --> 00:10:59.241
And in 1989,
the plant-based Kaiju,

00:10:59.325 --> 00:11:02.370
Biollante, joined the
Godzilla franchise.

00:11:02.453 --> 00:11:05.164
Since the 21st century,
killer plants in horror

00:11:05.247 --> 00:11:07.708
and science fiction narratives
have become more varied

00:11:07.792 --> 00:11:10.252
in appearance, origin,
and definition.

00:11:10.336 --> 00:11:12.463
From the more obvious
interpretations,

00:11:12.546 --> 00:11:14.382
creeping carnivorous vines

00:11:14.465 --> 00:11:16.759
and anthropomorphic
tree stump babies

00:11:16.842 --> 00:11:20.763
to unseen parasitic fungi that
cause humans to crave flesh

00:11:20.846 --> 00:11:25.101
and psychedelic alien spores,
we still have the tendency

00:11:25.184 --> 00:11:27.770
to transform nature into
something frightening.

00:11:27.853 --> 00:11:31.023
The biological systems of plants
are dramatically different

00:11:31.107 --> 00:11:32.733
from other life forms.

00:11:32.817 --> 00:11:34.235
Most make their own food

00:11:34.318 --> 00:11:36.445
and breathe what
we exhale as waste.

00:11:36.529 --> 00:11:38.823
It is more challenging
for us to draw similarities

00:11:38.906 --> 00:11:41.242
between flora than fauna.

00:11:41.325 --> 00:11:43.536
Even when we try to
make comparisons,

00:11:43.619 --> 00:11:45.955
like with the mandrake,
large duck orchid,

00:11:46.038 --> 00:11:47.832
or bleeding heart flowers,

00:11:47.915 --> 00:11:50.292
the analogy is
never fully formed.

00:11:50.376 --> 00:11:53.045
This extreme otherness
can heighten the terror

00:11:53.129 --> 00:11:56.006
when they turn monstrous,
exploiting our discomfort

00:11:56.090 --> 00:11:58.676
with living beings
whose very existence is

00:11:58.759 --> 00:12:00.803
so dramatically
different from our own.

00:12:00.886 --> 00:12:04.265
So next time you gaze lovingly
at the plant sitting serenely

00:12:04.348 --> 00:12:06.892
in the corners of your home,
spilling leaves

00:12:06.976 --> 00:12:09.228
over bookcase shelves
and silently swinging

00:12:09.311 --> 00:12:11.105
from macrame hammocks,

00:12:11.188 --> 00:12:13.607
remember that it is
in the quietness,

00:12:13.691 --> 00:12:15.234
that seeming meekness,

00:12:15.317 --> 00:12:17.736
that the killer plant
lies ready to strike.

00:12:17.820 --> 00:12:20.406
So have you talked
to your plants today?

00:12:20.489 --> 00:12:23.284
If you love the outdoors,
we think you'll really enjoy

00:12:23.367 --> 00:12:25.202
the newest digital
series from PBS

00:12:25.286 --> 00:12:27.913
called "America
Outdoors: Understory."

00:12:27.997 --> 00:12:29.582
Hosted by Baratunde Thurston,

00:12:29.665 --> 00:12:30.916
the show explores
our passionate,

00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:32.501
yet complex relationship

00:12:32.585 --> 00:12:34.044
with the natural world.

00:12:34.128 --> 00:12:35.504
Along the way, you
get to meet everyone

00:12:35.588 --> 00:12:38.132
from surfing scientists
to fossil hunters.

00:12:38.215 --> 00:12:39.258
There's a link in
the description.

00:12:39.341 --> 00:12:40.718
We hope you go check it out,

00:12:40.801 --> 00:12:43.345
and then maybe go
get some fresh air.

00:12:43.429 --> 00:12:44.513
(Speaker)
Rolling.

00:12:44.597 --> 00:12:45.639
What is that?

00:12:45.723 --> 00:12:47.141
Upas.

00:12:47.224 --> 00:12:48.058
Upas.

00:12:48.142 --> 00:12:49.351
(Speaker)
This one's native.

00:12:49.435 --> 00:12:50.644
Ooh.

00:12:50.728 --> 00:12:51.687
Plant.

00:12:51.770 --> 00:12:54.982
I really want a Venus
flytrap as a pet.

00:12:55.065 --> 00:12:56.525
Hey.

00:12:56.609 --> 00:12:58.861
(Speaker)
It's like an alien spaceship.

00:12:58.944 --> 00:13:00.279
Nyu nyu nyu.

00:13:00.362 --> 00:13:03.365
Accessibility provided by the
U.S. Department of Education.

